WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:02.770
Speaker 3: Hi.

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:12.769
I was just kind of wondering what you think
about what's going to happen in midterm elections?

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:20.250
I mean, I'm not looking too much at like polls
at this point, but just from my perception,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.429
I feel like Democrats are getting a lot less
popular.

00:00:23.429 --> 00:00:28.079
A lot of people, you know, are blaming Biden
for the gas bill or the gas prices.

00:00:28.079 --> 00:00:33.560
And I just feel like if they don't do anything
about it, they're going to stay very unpopular.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:39.430
I was wondering if you thought that they were
either aware of it or like what you would

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.430
recommend.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:45.829
Speaker 1: So I think Democrats are aware
of it.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:48.109
So so there's two sides to this.

00:00:48.109 --> 00:00:53.010
Austin, the first part of it is historically,
and if you can do something to turn off the

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.350
discord blinks, that would be really great.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:02.039
I don't know if you're using push to talk
or if you haven't silence notifications, but

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:04.640
I would love your help with that.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.980
So on the one hand, for historical reasons,
typically when a party wins the White House,

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.450
then the next midterms aren't good for them.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.910
So like historically speaking, Democrats should
should lose at least control of the House

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:16.910
or Senate in November.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:23.020
On the other hand, we have this situation
where in 2016 and 2020 you had a lot of Trumpists

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.020
voting who never voted before Trump.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.520
They only got involved in politics because
of Trump.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.320
And it remains an open question whether there
is going to be a sort of a disappearance of

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.720
many of those individuals, and they're not
even going to vote in November.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.670
The other question is we know COVID has killed
more Republicans than Democrats.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.510
To what effect will the Partizan death gap
of COVID affect Republicans, so Democrats

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.000
should be losing?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.080
Historically, Joe Biden is also getting blamed
for stuff that's not his fault, like gas prices.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.110
That's that those are not good things for
them.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.080
But I think it's such a unique midterm that
it remains to be seen.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.080
I don't really have a prediction.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.080
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.550
Do you think that though, like certain parts
like, I don't know, do you think, do you see

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.770
kind of the new like decriminalization of
marijuana bill is like something that they

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.129
might be putting forth to kind of reclaim
some popularity because I just feel like from

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:14.000
the perspective, like they have not really
gotten anything done with like the infrastructure

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.099
bill being gutted and they're not really doing
anything popular like as of right now or making

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:20.470
an effort to.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:27.090
Speaker 1: I think that time is quite short
and it would be really great for Democrats

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.480
if they want to do well to try to get something
else major done.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.129
But to be perfectly frank, it's not seeming
to me like they're they're going to be able

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:35.129
to do it.

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:43.080
So they're going to have to run on convincing
people based on what's already happened.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:52.999
And that's quite frankly, it seems like that's
going to be a difficult thing to do.

00:02:52.999 --> 00:02:57.719
Speaker 3: Yeah, I kind of just wish they
had more control over, you know, getting policies

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:02.139
that are popular, but obviously there's a
lot of, I guess, red tape within the party.

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:05.489
Speaker 1: Yeah, and there's also a limited
amount of political capital.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:10.689
The one thing I will say is I don't really
think Joe Biden has much of an interest in

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:15.439
doing student loan forgiveness, but he still
could look at doing something like that.

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:19.829
And maybe the new moratorium on payments until
August is him saying, Well, maybe in August,

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:23.200
I'll do some student loan forgiveness, which
will help us in November.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.160
That was Pat's idea on a recent bonus show.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.410
I don't know.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:34.230
I don't know, but I think it's an interesting
question.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.859
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:40.219
Well, thank you for your time, I definitely
agree that Biden should be doing some kind

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.510
of like emergency kind of catch up.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:51.370
I feel like you're just catching a lot of
flak for inflation and gas.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:57.419
And like, I obviously don't have like any
like concrete evidence on this.

00:03:57.419 --> 00:04:00.260
But just from what I've seen living in, like
Florida, I'm at the University of Florida,

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.299
but I'm from Miami.

00:04:02.299 --> 00:04:03.760
Just a lot of like A. Biden sentiment.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.290
Let's go, Brandon is everywhere.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:06.290
Yeah.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:10.120
I'm just a little worried for the future.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:16.030
Speaker 1: You're right to be worried.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:21.320
Austin, thank you so much for the call.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.990
Let's go next to Jamie from Georgia.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:23.700
Jamie, welcome.

